## Meteorological history
It continued to strengthen as it turned more to the northeast, and made landfall near the island of Cayo Costa, Florida as a 150 mph (240 km/h)Category 4 hurricane with a pressure of 941 mbar (941 hPa; 27.8 inHg) at approximately 1945 UTC on August 13.[[4]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hurricane_Charley#cite_note-hpc-4)[[8]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hurricane_Charley#cite_note-fsem-8) An hour later, the hurricane struck Punta Gorda as a 145 mph (235 km/h) storm and then passed up through Port Charlotte and the Charlotte Harbor.[[3]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hurricane_Charley#cite_note-tcr-3) The eye had shrunk before landfall, limiting the most powerful winds to an area within 7 mi (11 km) of the center.[[3]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hurricane_Charley#cite_note-tcr-3)
Charley weakened considerably due to its passage over land, but still retained sustained winds of about 85 mph (135 km/h) as it passed directly over Orlando between 0020 and 0140 UTC August 14;

